WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.743
Marcus’s PPO has a 1,500 dollar deductible, 80 percent in-network coinsurance,

00:00:05.743 --> 00:00:11.485
60 percent out of network and a 5,000 dollar out-of-pocket maximum.

00:00:11.485 --> 00:00:18.272
He is admitted to an in-network hospital with allowed charges of 28,000 dollars.

00:00:18.272 --> 00:00:20.360
Work it in order.

00:00:20.360 --> 00:00:21.926
Deductible first: 1,500.

00:00:21.926 --> 00:00:27.147
Coinsurance second: 20 percent of the remaining 26,500 is 5,300.

00:00:27.147 --> 00:00:32.368
Cap last: his total of 6,800 exceeds the 5,000 maximum,

00:00:32.368 --> 00:00:37.066
so he pays 5,000 and the plan pays 23,000.

00:00:37.066 --> 00:00:42.809
If the same stay had been out of network, the lower

00:00:42.809 --> 00:00:48.551
benefit level and any balance bill would apply, and the surprise

00:00:48.551 --> 00:00:53.250
billing rules might protect him only for emergency care.

00:00:53.250 --> 00:00:55.163
Rosa pays her disability premium quarterly.

00:00:55.163 --> 00:00:57.714
The premium due April 1 is not paid.

00:00:57.714 --> 00:01:00.902
Her policy allows the 31-day grace period that applies to

00:01:00.902 --> 00:01:03.772
quarterly premiums, so she is covered through May 1.

00:01:03.772 --> 00:01:05.685
On April 20 she is injured.

00:01:05.685 --> 00:01:08.235
The claim is covered, less the overdue premium.

00:01:08.235 --> 00:01:11.424
She must give notice within 20 days of the loss,

00:01:11.424 --> 00:01:14.612
the insurer must send claim forms within 15 days, and

00:01:14.612 --> 00:01:17.481
she must furnish proof of loss within 90 days.

00:01:17.481 --> 00:01:21.945
Had the injury come on May 15 after a lapse, she would need reinstatement,

00:01:21.945 --> 00:01:26.090
and a sickness beginning within ten days of reinstatement would not be covered.

00:01:26.090 --> 00:01:31.219
Rosa’s policy pays 3,600 dollars a month after a 90-day elimination period,

00:01:31.219 --> 00:01:36.348
with a residual benefit and a social insurance supplement of 1,200 dollars.

00:01:36.348 --> 00:01:39.340
She is totally disabled on June 1.

00:01:39.340 --> 00:01:42.760
Benefits accrue from August 30 and the first

00:01:42.760 --> 00:01:45.752
check arrives at the end of September.

00:01:45.752 --> 00:01:49.171
In month ten Social Security approves her for

00:01:49.171 --> 00:01:52.590
1,500 dollars a month, so the supplement stops.

00:01:52.590 --> 00:01:58.147
In month fourteen she returns half time with a 40 percent income loss;

00:01:58.147 --> 00:02:03.703
her residual benefit is 40 percent of 3,600, which is 1,440, and her

00:02:03.703 --> 00:02:09.260
waiver of premium continues as long as she remains disabled under the policy.

00:02:09.260 --> 00:02:12.777
Elena’s coworker at a 12-employee clinic is laid off

00:02:12.777 --> 00:02:15.902
on March 31 after two years of coverage.

00:02:15.902 --> 00:02:20.591
COBRA does not apply, because the employer has fewer than 20 employees.

00:02:20.591 --> 00:02:25.280
Maryland continuation does: she had more than three months of coverage and

00:02:25.280 --> 00:02:29.969
lost her job for a reason other than cause, so she may

00:02:29.969 --> 00:02:34.657
elect within 45 days to continue the group plan for up to

00:02:34.657 --> 00:02:38.565
18 months at the employer-plus-employee premium plus an approved fee.

00:02:38.565 --> 00:02:43.253
She may also convert to an individual policy within 31 days, or

00:02:43.253 --> 00:02:47.942
use a 60-day special enrollment period on Maryland Health Connection, where a

00:02:47.942 --> 00:02:52.240
premium tax credit may make a marketplace plan cheaper than continuation.

00:02:52.240 --> 00:02:58.537
Marcus, born in September, and Elena, born in March, both carry family coverage.

00:02:58.537 --> 00:03:01.928
Their son’s allowed charges are 6,000 dollars.

00:03:01.928 --> 00:03:05.804
Elena’s plan is primary under the birthday rule

00:03:05.804 --> 00:03:09.679
and pays 4,200 after its deductible and coinsurance.

00:03:09.679 --> 00:03:15.008
Marcus’s plan is secondary and would have paid 4,800 alone, so

00:03:15.008 --> 00:03:19.852
it pays the 1,800 balance and the family owes nothing.

00:03:19.852 --> 00:03:25.665
In July Marcus’s employer changes carriers; a deductible carryover credits what he

00:03:25.665 --> 00:03:31.478
already paid, and no-loss no-gain keeps his son’s ongoing orthopedic treatment covered.

00:03:31.478 --> 00:03:38.260
In November Elena’s hours drop below the eligible class, triggering her own continuation rights.

00:03:38.260 --> 00:03:40.040
Three clients, three designs.

00:03:40.040 --> 00:03:43.599
Priya’s younger employees want the lowest premium and

00:03:43.599 --> 00:03:46.714
accept a network and referrals: an HMO.

00:03:46.714 --> 00:03:50.274
Marcus wants to keep a specialist outside any

00:03:50.274 --> 00:03:53.388
network and will pay more: a PPO.

00:03:53.388 --> 00:03:57.838
Rosa wants HMO pricing most of the time with the

00:03:57.838 --> 00:04:02.287
option to go outside when it matters: a point-of-service plan.

00:04:02.287 --> 00:04:05.402
For each, the questions are the same.

00:04:05.402 --> 00:04:07.182
Is a referral required?

00:04:07.182 --> 00:04:09.407
Is there any out-of-network benefit?

00:04:09.407 --> 00:04:11.631
Are copayments or coinsurance used?

00:04:11.631 --> 00:04:17.861
Add the Lesson 19 arithmetic and the Lesson 23 coordination rules, and most plan

00:04:17.861 --> 00:04:24.090
questions on the exam become a matter of reading carefully and working in order.
